      "narrationText": "Avocado Oil!\n\n-\n\nRanked!\n\n-\n\n100 grams of fat. Omega 3 is 957 milligrams, doing a lot of the work here. For this category, the real question is fat quality.\n\n-\n\n0 grams of carbs. The rest is not doing much. Not relevant here.\n\n-\n\n0 grams of protein. Everything else is fairly underwhelming. Also not relevant here.\n\n-\n\nVitamin e at 12% daily value, vitamin k at 14% daily value stand out most. That is barely moving the needle.\n\n-\n\nMinerals are basically not adding much here.\n\n-\n\nOn the plus side: small amounts can change a whole meal. That leverage matters when the fat quality is good. Highly useful in cooking. Practical culinary value matters here. Helps meals actually work. A fat that people can use well matters more than a theoretical one.\n\n-\n\nOn the weaker side: calorie density is brutally high. Tiny serving errors matter here. Easy to overpour without noticing. Practical portion creep is real. Easy to overuse without noticing. Liquid calories are slippery in the kitchen.\n\n-\n\nAvocado Oil has a genuinely strong overall case. The practical downsides are real, but they do not outweigh the upside. Best in controlled amounts where fat quality is the main reason to use it.\n\n-\n\nA tier.\n"
